    "q-4743": {
      "dom_id": "step-q4743",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14209655/assets/61dc032461e469146f11eacac4f917e86d40b3078dc2d33ac89e8e0b31eefd48.png)\n\nRounded to one decimal place, $x≈$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we are given the lengths of the adjacent side and the hypotenuse, we can use cosine to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{10}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $x$ using inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{10}{27} \\\\ \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{10}{27}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{10}{27}) \\\\ x & =(68.261\\,5…)^{∘} \\\\ x & ≈{68.3}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
